the distributed memory model I have in mind uses

-> entry consistency (so no placing locks around code instead locks are associated with data which is where they rightly belong)

There are 4 types of shared memory:

<a> read/writeable

<b> write never (readonly)

<c> write once  (publication safety)
    e.g., C++ / Java vtables

<d> read often  
    (fenceless reads, reader writer locks, low resolution timers)


Updates propogations are done lazily and only diffs are sent out. I m toying with doing striped update propogation (essentially 
sequentially written data such as those belonging to a shared block are kept on separate nearby servers to improve bandwidth
only when their size exceeds a certain limit)

Compression needs to be factored in too.

